# **Line endings are LF in the working tree, on every platform.**
#
# Windows git defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites every line on
# checkout. That is ordinarily harmless and here it is not: a cluster of tests
# in `tangent/src/desktop.rs` and `tangent/src/instrument.rs` reads its OWN
# SOURCE TEXT and asserts on byte offsets into it, because the thing being
# checked is an ORDERING that no behavioural test can see (see
# `a_device_change_forgets_what_the_decoder_refused`). One extra byte per line
# moves every offset.
#
# Measured on Windows 11, same commit, the `tangent` binary only:
#
#     core.autocrlf=true    1009 passed; 16 failed
#     core.autocrlf=false   1020 passed;  5 failed
#
# Eleven pure checkout artefacts, and the giveaway was an offset landing inside
# a multi-byte character: "end byte index 162500 is not a char boundary; it is
# inside '—'". Without this file a Windows contributor's first run is red for a
# reason that has nothing to do with their change.
* text=auto eol=lf

# **The vendored copies keep the bytes upstream shipped.**
#
# `tangent-record/vendor/asio-sys` is stored CRLF in the index because that is
# how upstream wrote it, and `vendor/README.md` turns byte-comparability with
# upstream into the argument for every patch in these trees. Normalising them
# would rewrite thirteen files and break exactly the property the vendor
# READMEs rest on. `-text` means "never convert, in either direction".
tangent-record/vendor/asio-sys/** -text
tangent-record/vendor/cpal/.gitignore -text

# Binary, so never transformed and never diffed as text.
*.wav   binary
*.aiff  binary
*.flac  binary
*.mp3   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.icns  binary
*.ico   binary
*.tan   binary
*.vst3  binary
*.pdf   binary
